{"name":"Confection","hex":"#f4ecda","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"confection","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A food item prepared very sweet, frequently decorated in fine detail, and often preserved with sugar, such as a candy, sweetmeat, fruit preserve, pastry, or cake.","The act or process of confecting; the process of making, compounding, or preparing something."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To make into a confection, prepare as a confection."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/confection"}],"entries":[{"title":"Confection (Sébastien Tellier album)","blocks":[{"text":"Confection is the fifth studio album by French musician Sébastien Tellier, released on 14 October 2013 by Record Makers."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/3/31/Confection_%28S%C3%A9bastien_Tellier_album%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confection_(S%C3%A9bastien_Tellier_album)","wikidata":"Q137862943"},{"title":"Confectionery","blocks":[{"text":"Confectionery is the art of making confections, or sweet foods. Confections are items that are rich in sugar and carbohydrates, although exact definitions are difficult. In general, however, confections are divided into two broad and somewhat overlapping categories: baker's confections and sugar confections."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Krokan.jpg/330px-Krokan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Confectionery","wikidata":"Q5159627"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Yasnaya_Polyana_Confectionery_Factory_01.jpg/500px-Yasnaya_Polyana_Confectionery_Factory_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Yasnaya_Polyana_Confectionery_Factory_01.jpg/500px-Yasnaya_Polyana_Confectionery_Factory_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Confectionery can be mass-produced in a factory.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYasnaya_Polyana_Confectionery_Factory_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Chum_chums.jpg/500px-Chum_chums.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Chum_chums.jpg/500px-Chum_chums.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Some Indian confectionery desserts from hundreds of varieties. In certain parts of India, these are called mithai or sweets. Sugar and desserts have a long history in India: by about 500 BCE, people in India had developed the technology to produce sugar crystals. In the local language, these crystals were called khanda (खण्ड), which is the source of the word candy.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChum_chums.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/La-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg/500px-La-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/La-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg/1280px-La-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/La-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg/500px-La-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jordan almonds. Sugar-coated nuts or spices for non-medicinal purposes marked the beginning of confectionery in late medieval England.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALa-Pone-Jordan-Almonds.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Pink_and_white_Easter_petits_fours.jpg/500px-Pink_and_white_